WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
UTORRENT:
Once upon a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:08.200
uTorrent was basically synonymous with torrents.&nbsp;
It was the go to place to download movies, music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.120
games, or something a bit more mature. Fun&nbsp;
fact, it was actually owned by Spotify in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.640
the early days who bought the company to learn&nbsp;
about their peer to peer distribution system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.600
Spotify would end up selling off uTorrent as they&nbsp;
wanted to disassociate from the world of piracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.800
but this didn’t stop uTorrent from exploding&nbsp;
in popularity anyway. Till just 3 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.720
they held nearly 70% market share within the&nbsp;
torrenting world with second place coming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:41.120
at just a tenth of the market share at 6.6%.&nbsp;
The funny part is that 2nd place, BitTorrent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.720
is actually the creator of BitTorrents&nbsp;
but they never held a candle against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:52.240
popularity of uTorrent which at their peak&nbsp;
translated to over a 150 million users. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.960
while uTorrent still controls a dominating share&nbsp;
of the market, the overall pie has not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:04.640
shrunk but it has become a sliver. Take Google&nbsp;
Trends for example. The search interest around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:10.240
uTorrent has basically been non existent for the&nbsp;
past 7 years with current interest standing at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.960
just 3% of its former peak. uTorrent has very&nbsp;
much trying to fight this trend. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.840
they launched a web version of uTorrent a couple&nbsp;
of years ago. And while that did reach a 1 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:28.960
daily active users, it’s nothing in comparison to&nbsp;
the glory days. uTorrent is very much feeling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.680
pain and at this point, it seems like they’re&nbsp;
just fighting for survival. They’ve stuffed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.200
ads into their UI and introduced paid tiers in&nbsp;
hopes of keeping the effort worthwhile. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.560
with interest in uTorrent continuing to dwindle,&nbsp;
this seems like an increasingly uphill battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.840
That’s why when uTorrent temporarily went down&nbsp;
in early 2023, many speculated if that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.680
end of uTorrent altogether. Fortunately&nbsp;
for uTorrent and its remaining users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:03.880
this wasn’t the case but it did give a glimpse&nbsp;
into the inevitable future. But, what happened?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:09.840
How did everyone forget about a once ubiquitous&nbsp;
service with over a hundred million users? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:23.506
join me as we take a look back at the monumental&nbsp;
rise and fall of the king of torrents.

00:02:23.506 --> 00:02:26.280
AN UNEXPECTED SUCCESS:
uTorrent very much popularized the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.560
downloading files in a decentralized manner. And&nbsp;
companies are currently popularizing the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.200
raising money in a decentralized manner with the&nbsp;
help of bonds. If you’re not familiar with bonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.320
it’s essentially way for governments and companies&nbsp;
to raise money from the general public as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.600
other companies and financial institutions.&nbsp;
These companies and governments will then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.360
turn around and use this money to fund expansion&nbsp;
efforts, research and development, and growth. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.840
in return for helping them, they’ll pay you back&nbsp;
with interest rates of currently 4-7%. Investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.720
like Warren Buffett are currently buying bonds&nbsp;
hand over first and locking in historically high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.000
interest rates for the long term. If you’d like to&nbsp;
do the same thing with the safety of $500,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.640
SIPC insurance and $2.5 million in FDIC insurance,&nbsp;
please consider checking out our bond investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:24.040
app, Silo, in the description below. But anyway,&nbsp;
getting back to uTorrent, the story of uTorrent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:30.000
takes us back to the early 2000s to a Swedish&nbsp;
programmer named Ludvig Strigeus. Ludvig never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.480
had any aspirations of creating something&nbsp;
massive or being in the spotlight. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.880
just a programmer who liked solving issues&nbsp;
that he came across on a day to day basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.600
and uTorrent was one of these creations. You see,&nbsp;
Ludvig was involved within the torrenting space in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:50.280
the early 2000s and he couldn’t stand that most&nbsp;
bittorrents came along with a bunch of bloatware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:56.440
and were resource hogs. So, in 2004, he started&nbsp;
working on a no frills bittorrent in his free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:02.960
time. This was very much an on and off thing. In&nbsp;
fact, after working on it for just a month, Ludvig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.240
would lose interest and sideline the project for&nbsp;
nearly a year. But, he did eventually come back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.800
it in September of 2005 with a new vigor. In fact,&nbsp;
he would finish off the program in just 3 days and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:21.280
launch uTorrent on September 18, 2005. Speaking&nbsp;
of the name, if you look closely at the logo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.520
you’ll see that the first letter isn’t exactly a&nbsp;
u. Personally, when I was a kid, I always thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.560
that this was just a stylized cursive u but&nbsp;
this is actually a greek letter called mu. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.720
the computing world, mu usually refers to micro,&nbsp;
so you could pronounce uTorrent as muTorrent or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.240
microTorrent and this is actually the case in&nbsp;
many countries. If this is true your country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.840
comment down below where you’re from. But,&nbsp;
for most users, including Ludvig himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.760
the mu looked like a u and thus it became known&nbsp;
as uTorrent. Within just a few months of launch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.320
hundreds of thousands of people would end up&nbsp;
downloading the client, and Ludvig would continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:04.960
working on uTorrent. For example, uTorrent would&nbsp;
become one of the first clients to implement DHT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.280
support and BitTorrent encryption. But, while&nbsp;
these were nice additions, the main factor that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:15.640
sold uTorrent was its light weight design. This&nbsp;
didn’t just catch the eye of pirates through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:22.480
it also caught the eye of Daniel Ek, the future&nbsp;
CEO of Spotify. Back in 2005 though, Daniel was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.880
just another Swedish programmer who was fascinated&nbsp;
by the innerworkings of uTorrent and especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:34.280
Ludvig. You see, Daniel had already begun working&nbsp;
on Spotify and they were figuring out the hard way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:39.760
that pursuing a server client model for streaming&nbsp;
music woud be extraordinarily expensive. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:44.480
instead, they wanted to leverage a light weight&nbsp;
peer to peer model and who better to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:50.080
them with this than Ludwig. With that, Spotify&nbsp;
would purchase uTorrent for an undisclosed amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.920
which was likely less than a million dollars.&nbsp;
This means that Spotify’s CEO was technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:01.720
also uTorrent’s CEO at one point in time but this&nbsp;
didn’t last all that long. As Spotify’s CTO put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:08.080
it, “we bought uTorrent, but what we really wanted&nbsp;
was Ludvig.” As such, as soon as they were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.480
convince Ludvig to stick around at Spotify,&nbsp;
they would turn around and sell uTorrent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:17.560
BitTorrent behind closed doors. It wasn’t till&nbsp;
years later that it would be revealed that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:24.200
sellers were actually Spotify. This was basically&nbsp;
the end of Ludvig’s involvement with uTorrent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:29.080
looking back, this was a great decision as&nbsp;
Ludvig would end up with a $100 million when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:44.022
Spotify IPOed. But, while Ludvig had already&nbsp;
moved on, uTorrent was just getting started.

00:06:44.022 --> 00:06:46.160
REACHING THE MOON:
While Ludvig was no longer involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:51.640
the truth was that uTorrent had been turned&nbsp;
over to someone even more capable: Bram Cohen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:56.400
If you’re not familiar with Bram, he is the&nbsp;
father of torrenting as he created the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.760
first peer 2 peer BitTorrent based file sharing&nbsp;
program. He aptly named the program BitTorrent but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:08.600
while BitTorrent was the OG, Bram could see that&nbsp;
uTorrent was OP. It was faster, more efficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:14.760
and had a better UI, but most importantly,&nbsp;
it was rapidly growing in popularity. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:22.560
armed with VC funding, Bram would take the plunge&nbsp;
with uTorrent but not everyone was happy. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.560
Bram didn’t exactly create torrents with piracy&nbsp;
in mind. Obviously, he knew that it woud happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.880
but his main goal with torrents wasn’t to enable&nbsp;
piracy, it was to make torrents a mainstream way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:40.120
to download pretty much anything. And as such, it&nbsp;
was important that Bram was on the right side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:46.800
the law. So, in late 2005, he would go ahead and&nbsp;
strike a deal with the MPAA or the Motion Picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.360
Association of America. This deal entailed that&nbsp;
Bram would remove all copyrighted material from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:58.600
the official BitTorrent search engine. This turned&nbsp;
a lot of the torrenting community against Bram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:03.720
as much of the torrenting community was also&nbsp;
pro piracy. Moreover, much of the torrenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:10.160
community was in favor of a decentralized network.&nbsp;
By purchasing uTorrent though, Bram was basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:15.800
monopolizing the torrenting space. So, the core&nbsp;
torrenting community wasn’t happy with any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:22.240
this but as for the general public, well, they&nbsp;
really didn’t care or even know about any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:28.040
this. As far as they were concerned, uTorrent&nbsp;
was by far the best BitTorrent client and Bram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:33.800
was simply making it more stable and accessible.&nbsp;
Also, about Bram taking down copyrighted torrents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.400
well this simply made way for the Pirate Bay,&nbsp;
Kick Ass Torrents, and a slew of other search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:44.760
engines for torrents leading us into the glory&nbsp;
days of uTorrent. By 2008, uTorrent boasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:52.440
over 25 million active users and it was basically&nbsp;
synonymous with torrents but there was one major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:58.800
problem: money. All of this was funded using&nbsp;
investor capital meaning that sooner or later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:04.280
investors would want to see a return. This&nbsp;
was very much a tricky line to walk as doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:09.440
anything over the top would simply push away&nbsp;
users and make way for a new BitTorrent. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.880
the company started off simple. They would&nbsp;
start bundling in optional toolbars into their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:19.240
installations. They would also add in a Google&nbsp;
powered torrent search. These actually brought in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:24.840
a pretty decent amount of revenue, an estimated&nbsp;
$15 to $20 million per year. But with a 125&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:31.120
million users to support and the 2008 financial&nbsp;
recession, it simply wasn’t enough. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:37.000
BitTorrent would be forced to do layoffs and close&nbsp;
parts of their business. But despite the losses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:43.120
BitTorrent was able to scrap by mostly thanks to&nbsp;
just how stupidly popular uTorrent was. Investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.400
weren’t gonna just let a platform with over&nbsp;
a hundred million active users go under just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:53.520
because of a funding issue. So, they helped keep&nbsp;
BitTorrent afloat but see, this only works as long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:59.120
as uTorrent is growing at an unprecedented&nbsp;
rate which we know wouldn’t last forever.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:07.080
THE UNGRACEFUL FALL:
The truth is that much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:12.440
of uTorrent’s fall from grace wasn’t even their&nbsp;
fault. The reality is that interest in torrents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:17.040
has simply fallen substantially over the past&nbsp;
decade thanks to more affordable legal options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:22.440
entering the market. Ironically, one of these&nbsp;
options is of course Spotify which has largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:28.680
made music piracy a thing of the past. So, by&nbsp;
helping Spotify succeed, Ludvig actually ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:34.920
up killing his original invention which is quite&nbsp;
poetic actually but Spotify was just one factor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.960
This same trend occurred in basically every&nbsp;
copyrighted industry. You no longer had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:46.240
$40 to $60 per month just to watch TV shows and&nbsp;
with a bunch of ads. Instead, you could just pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:52.000
$10 per month to Netflix and get access to a&nbsp;
far larger library whenever you want. The same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.680
thing could be said about gaming as well. Many of&nbsp;
the most popular games of the 2010s whether it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:03.720
Clash of Clans or Fortnite were completely free&nbsp;
to play. They made their money based on in game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:09.480
purchases making it useless to pirate these games.&nbsp;
Also, as for the games that were still paid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:13.440
well, a lot of these were dependent on online&nbsp;
functionality which you couldn’t really get if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:19.040
you pirated. And as for software, well most&nbsp;
software just became free as they learned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.600
monetize our use as opposed to our purchase.&nbsp;
Just as all of this was starting to unfold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:30.000
some of the biggest piracy sites would be taken&nbsp;
down. This included Megaupload in 2012 and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.280
Pirate Bay in 2014. Of course, replacements&nbsp;
would pop up to take their place but these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:41.920
never reached the same scale because people simply&nbsp;
didn’t feel the need for these services. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:47.240
just take a look at this graph showcasing the&nbsp;
interest around downloading free movies. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:52.680
piracy began to lose steam, it was only natural&nbsp;
that uTorrent followed suit making them more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:58.280
more desperate. First, they would add ads in&nbsp;
late 2012. Then, they would add a pro tier for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:03.920
$20 per year which kind of nerfed the free tier.&nbsp;
These changes definitely made the free version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:09.920
uTorrent less appealing but these changes were&nbsp;
understandable. At the end of the day uTorrent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:15.160
was a business that needed to make money. But,&nbsp;
a change that the community would not tolerate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:22.200
however, was deception. In early 2015, uTorrent&nbsp;
would begin secretly installing a cryptocurrency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:28.160
miner onto user’s computers in order to mine&nbsp;
litecoin and generate more revenue. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:33.280
was this bloatware but it was rather shady&nbsp;
given that uTorrent was very much trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:39.520
do it under the radar. And with that, uTorrent&nbsp;
had officially been around long enough to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:46.160
themselves become the villain and this pretty much&nbsp;
sealed the fate for uTorrent. The core torrenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:50.280
community would move onto other services like&nbsp;
qBitTorrent while the general public would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:56.280
ditch torrenting altogether leading us into&nbsp;
uTorrent’s current situation. For years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:02.760
BitTorrent has been shutting down service after&nbsp;
service after service, so uTorrent may very well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:08.600
be on the chopping block next. The financials&nbsp;
of uTorrent aren’t public, so it’s difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:14.680
judge the financial viability of uTorrent. Maybe&nbsp;
uTorrent is breakeven or even slightly making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:20.280
profit, it’s not clear. But, what is clear is&nbsp;
that the glory days of uTorrent are long behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:25.840
us and all we have left is a shell of their&nbsp;
formerselves. Someone else that went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:30.840
a similar journey is the Pirate Bay. If you’d like&nbsp;
to hear about their story, check out this video.

